{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What does `def` do?",
      "options": [
        "It defines a function: a named block of code we can run again and again",
        "It runs a function right away",
        "It prints the lines under it once",
        "It makes a variable called def"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "What does this program print?",
      "code": "def greet():\n    print(\"Hi\")",
      "options": [
        "Hi",
        "greet",
        "Nothing: the function is defined but never called",
        "It stops with an error"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "Which line runs a function called `greet` that takes nothing?",
      "options": ["def greet()", "greet()", "greet", "print(greet)"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "What has to come at the end of a `def` line?",
      "options": [";", ".", ",", ":"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "In `def my_function(name, age):`, what are `name` and `age`?",
      "options": [
        "Two variables that have to exist before the function",
        "The function's name, in two parts",
        "The function's parameters: they hold the values it is called with",
        "Two texts the function prints as they are"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "With `def my_function(name, age):`, the call is `my_function(\"Cortez\", 60)`. What does `age` hold?",
      "options": ["60", "\"Cortez\"", "\"Cortez\" and 60", "Nothing, it has to be set first"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "Can we name a function's parameters as we like?",
      "options": [
        "No: they have to be name and age",
        "Yes: the function uses those names inside it, whatever they are",
        "No: they have to match variables outside the function",
        "Only with one letter, like n and a"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "def show(name, age):\n    print(f\"{name} is {age}\")\n\nshow(42, \"Tommy\")",
      "options": ["Tommy is 42", "name is age", "It stops with an error", "42 is Tommy"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "Why does the lesson put the for loop in a function instead of copying it?",
      "options": [
        "A function runs faster than a for loop",
        "A for loop can only run inside a function",
        "We write it once and call it as often as we like, with other values",
        "So the loop runs only once"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "How many times does this print Hi?",
      "code": "def hi():\n    print(\"Hi\")\n\nhi()\nhi()\nhi()",
      "options": ["3", "1", "4", "0"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "greet()\n\ndef greet():\n    print(\"Hello\")",
      "options": [
        "It prints Hello",
        "It stops with an error: greet doesn't exist yet when it is called",
        "It prints nothing",
        "It prints Hello twice"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "def add(a, b):\n    print(a + b)\n\nadd(5)",
      "options": [
        "It prints 5",
        "It prints 5 + b",
        "It prints nothing",
        "It stops with an error: b gets no value"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "Which lines belong to a function?",
      "options": [
        "Every line after def, to the end of the program",
        "Only the first line under def",
        "The indented lines under its def line",
        "The lines where it is called"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "In the lesson, what does `f(\"Admin\", False)` print?",
